n and capital-services terms. OECD-38 LEG = 0.90%/yr.\nCHINA LEG. Labour input -1.1%/yr x labour share 0.52 = -0.57pp; capital services +4.0%/yr x capital share 0.48 = +1.92pp (property-driven deepening exhausted, ICOR rising); TFP +1.40pp; NBS measurement wedge +0.40pp (I forecast the MEASURED series). Sum = 3.15. Cross-check against the locked -2.7pp/yr China aging drag: un-aged trend ~5.2% less a drag that is back-loaded over 2025-2050 and therefore ~-2.2pp realized by 2032-36 -> 3.0%. CHINA LEG = 2.90%/yr.\nCOMBINED. 0.63(0.90) + 0.37(2.90) = 0.567 + 1.073 = 1.640 -> P50 1.65%/yr.\nTHE WEIGHT x DRAG INTERACTION, STATED AS THE BRIEF REQUIRES. China's 0.37 weight times its -2.7pp drag = -1.00pp on the combined aggregate by itself. The OECD leg's own aging drag (per-capita -0.40 plus labour-force contribution ~-0.45) is ~-0.85pp at a 0.63 weight = -0.54pp. Total aging drag on THIS aggregate = -1.54pp, which is ~40% LARGER than 2A's global -1.1pp figure, purely because the OECD-38 + China geography excludes India and Africa. Any panelist who imports the global -1.1pp into this question understates the drag by ~0.44pp. That is the single largest arithmetic finding in my CQ3.\nDISPERSION. A 5-year annualized average compresses tails; sd ~0.65-0.70pp. Recession hazard inside the window 45% at a cost of 0.5-0.7pp on the average. Left-skewed because the binding constraint (direct personal care hours for the 80+ cohort; grid interconnection and firm capacity) is a cap, not a symmetric shock. P10 0.8% (bust + care rationing + BIS Box C demand deficit + China under 2%); P90 2.6% (no bust, Transformative Breakout tail, China 4%, migration liberalisation relieving the physical wall). Implied: P(>=2.6) = 0.10, P(<2.0) = 0.68, P(<1.7) = 0.53 — carried verbatim into CQ6.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.654Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsificat